- Used when problem has exponential(or factorial) number of possible states - 
so the search space is to large to do an exhaustive search.
- Tries to avoid local optimum and finds global optimum instead.
- TSP and TrainingNN
- Temperature (T) reduces over time. When temp is high, we should accept bad moves in the 
beginning of the algorithm and later on reduce the probability of bad moves.
If T is low, keep accepting good moves. T controls what states to accept and consider - may even 
accept bad states as well.
- At each iteration, a new candidate state is generated and its distance from 
the current point is proprotional to temperature.
- If new point is better, it replaces the current point as the actual best so far.
- It is possible to accept and move fwd with a worst state and the probability of 
doing so is directly dependent on T and it may help to find a new search region.

